Time and Space Complexity
Time Complexity: O(n), where n is the length of the array. We iterate over the array once, and each operation (checking the hash map and updating it) takes constant time.

Space Complexity: O(n), where n is the length of the array. In the worst case, all cumulative sums could be unique, and the hash map would store up to n entries.

Summary Table:
Metric	Value
Time Complexity	O(n)
Space Complexity	O(n)
Advantages of Hashing in This Problem:
Efficient Lookup: The use of the hash map allows us to check if a subarray sum equals K in constant time (O(1)).

Avoid Recalculating Sums: Without hashing, we would need to compute sums for all subarrays, which would take O(n^2) time. The hash map prevents redundant calculations and improves the solution to O(n).
